1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,720
 So here is an example problem to solve.

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:09,200
 Now, none of the mailbox, run of the routers, none of the post office offices know exactly

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:12,880
 where the thing is going and they don't transport it to the final destination.

4
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:17,320
 All they do is they transport it to the future, to a further down destination.

5
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:18,960
 But what if they get it wrong?

6
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:24,440
 What if one, this is like Michigan, this is Chicago, this is Dallas, and what if Michigan

7
00:00:24,440 --> 00:00:28,240
 thinks sending to Chicago is a good idea, Chicago thinks sending to Dallas is a good

8
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:31,600
 idea, and then Dallas thinks sending to Michigan is a good idea?

9
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:34,960
 Well, then Michigan's going to get it again and send it back to Chicago.

10
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:40,960
 And so then what you end up with is this situation where your packet, your data, is in a loop.

11
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:41,960
 Right?

12
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:45,880
 This wouldn't be good for the post office and it's not good for the internet.

13
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:49,840
 So you have to say, how do we solve this problem when something's wrong?

14
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:50,840
 Right?

15
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:53,880
 It's, you wish it were perfect, but it's not.

16
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:58,380
 And so this is the kind of research question as to how to solve this kind of problem.

17
00:00:58,380 --> 00:01:02,440
 And we'll talk more about this in a bit when we get into a little more technical detail.

18
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:06,640
 But I just wanted to sort of say, that's the kind of research and engineering that took

19
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:11,440
 literally 20 years and four different versions of the ARPANET to get right.

